fn parse_and_sort(
    context: &mut Context,
    seq: &[Element],
    sorted: bool,
    ascending: bool,
    writer: &mut Writer,
) -> Result<(), Error> {
    if !sorted {
        for item in seq {
            context.parse(writer, r#type.element(), item)?;
        }
    } else {
        let mut elements = Vec::with_capacity(seq.len());
        for item in seq {
            let element_vec = context.parse_to_vec(r#type.element(), item)?;
            elements.push(element_vec);
        }
        sort(context, r#type.element(), ascending, &mut elements)?;
        for element in &elements {
            writer.write_all(element)?;
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

In the solution, the `parse_and_sort` function first checks the `sorted` flag. If `sorted` is false, it iterates through the sequence and parses each element using the `context.parse` method. If `sorted` is true, it first parses each element to a vector using the `context.parse_to_vec` method, then sorts the vectors using the `sort` method, and finally writes the sorted elements to the `writer`. The function returns `Ok(())` upon successful completion.

