#include "Context.Editor.NewRoom.h"
```

Your task is to implement the function:
```cpp
int countUniqueHeaderFiles(const std::vector<std::string>& includedFiles);
```
where `includedFiles` is a vector of strings representing the included header files. The function should return the count of unique header file names.

For example, given the input:
```cpp
{"Graphics.Layouts.h", "Common.Sounds.h", "Options.h", "Game.Descriptors.h", "Game.ScenarioDescriptors.h", "map", "Context.Editor.NewRoom.h"}
```
the function should return `6`, as there are six unique header file names: "Graphics.Layouts.h", "Common.Sounds.h", "Options.h", "Game.Descriptors.h", "Game.ScenarioDescriptors.h", and "Context.Editor.NewRoom.h".

Assume that the input vector `includedFiles` may contain both standard library headers (e.g., `<map>`) and user-defined headers (e.g., "Graphics.Layouts.h").

response:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_set>

int countUniqueHeaderFiles(const std::vector<std::string>& includedFiles) {
    std::unordered_set<std::string> uniqueFiles;

    for (const std::string& file : includedFiles) {
        size_t pos = file.find_last_of("/\\");  // Find the last path separator
        std::string baseName = (pos == std::string::npos) ? file : file.substr(pos + 1);
        uniqueFiles.insert(baseName);
    }

    return uniqueFiles.size();
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> includedFiles = {"Graphics.Layouts.h", "Common.Sounds.h", "Options.h", "Game.Descriptors.h", "Game.ScenarioDescriptors.h", "map", "Context.Editor.NewRoom.h"};
    std::cout << countUniqueHeaderFiles(includedFiles) << std::endl;  // Output: 6
    return 0;
}
```

The `countUniqueHeaderFiles` function uses an `unordered_set` to store the unique base file names of the included header files. It iterates through the input vector, extracts the base file names, and inserts them into the set. Finally, it returns the size of the set, which represents the count of unique header file names.

